WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:27.760
[Music]

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:39.000
[Siren sounds, ambulance speeds through the night. ]

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:49.440
[Intercom:] Dr. Robbins, Dr. Robbins, emergency.
Dr. Robbins, Dr. Robbins, Dr. Robbins.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:57.280
[Health care workers accompany a stretcher with a patient on it down a hospital corridor.]

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:07.380
[Patient is rolled into Emergency Room and doors are closed.]

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:24.660
[The hands of the clock above the doors show time passing. The doors open.]

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.100
[Jerry:] Well, is she going to make it doctor?

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:31.820
[Dr. Robbins:] Hello, Jerry. Well, how's the
world's greatest newspaper man this morning?

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:33.840
[Jerry:] Okay doctor, I'm still looking for
news. 

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:36.200
Say, about the youngster, is she going to be all right?

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:38.880
[Dr. Robbins:] Yes Jerry. No tragic ending
this time. 

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:43.060
This little girl's going to get well, thanks to Dr. Hartley here.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:45.140
[Dr. Hartley:] Well, you're too generous doctor.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.860
The real lifesaving was done before she  got to the hospital.

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:50.460
[Jerry:] Well, I don't get it doctor. What's
the story?

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:54.260
[Dr. Hartley:] Here's what I mean, Jerry.
The real hero was somebody whose name you

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:58.300
and I will never know. The good samaritan
that gave that little girl first aid

00:01:58.300 --> 00:01:59.599
at the scene of the accident.

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:04.220
[Dr. Robbins:] Yes Jerry, that person was
one in a thousand.

00:02:04.229 --> 00:02:08.980
[Jerry:] Oh, but people always try to help.
That is, most of them do. Now, you can't expect--

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:15.860
[Dr. Robbins:] Yes, I know Jerry. Most people are willing to help, but that alone isn't enough.

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:18.920
It takes something more than just
willingness to save a life.

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:22.620
[Jerry:] Well, after all we can't all be doctors.

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:26.220
[Dr. Robbins:] Well, you don't have to be.
But, if everyone who is willing to help in

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:34.570
an accident or emergency were armed with just
a little, well, just the bare basic essentials

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:39.890
of first aid, a great many lives would be
saved.

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:50.790
If everyone...if you, and you, and you, and
you, would take the time to learn

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:57.860
what to do before the doctor arrived.

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:04.120
Know what to look for. Know what to do. Know
how to do it.

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:10.500
These are the essentials of first aid. Now,
let's see how these principles apply,

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:15.080
first in regard to wounds.

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:28.060
A sharp-edged knife, a broken bottle, a sharp
piece of metal, a shattered windshield...

00:03:28.069 --> 00:03:34.420
they are but a few of the causes of wounds, wounds that bleed.

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:40.880
Bleeding can be fatal, but it can be stopped
effectively with simple first aid treatment.

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:45.940
There are two main types of bleeding that
can occur.

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:52.080
Bleeding from arteries and bleeding from veins.
To recognize each type you should know how

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:55.790
the blood circulates in the body.

00:03:55.790 --> 00:04:02.220
The heart acts as a pumping station. The right
side of the heart pumps blood to the lungs.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:09.220
In the lungs the blood picks up oxygen, which
is necessary to sustain life.

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:13.610
The blood then returns to the left side of
the heart, where it is pumped to all parts

00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:19.799
of the body through a system of tubes known as arteries, then through a network of tiny

00:04:19.799 --> 00:04:24.359
blood vessels called capillaries, and back
again to the heart through

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:28.479
another system of vessels, the veins.

00:04:28.479 --> 00:04:34.819
When one of the arteries is cut, bleeding
is serious. Have you ever seen the way water

00:04:34.819 --> 00:04:39.859
spurts from a hand pump? Well, that is the
way blood spurts from an artery

00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:42.300
which has been cut.

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:49.190
Blood spurts with force from the wound each
time the heart beats. 

00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:53.240
This type of bleeding must be stopped quickly to save life.

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:58.560
In most cases bleeding can be stopped effectively
by simple first aid measures

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:03.000
such as pressure applied directly on the wound.

00:05:03.009 --> 00:05:10.009
If this does not stop the bleeding apply finger
pressure, sometimes called digital pressure,

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:12.420
between the heart and the wound.

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:20.260
Another way is to use a tourniquet similarly
placed. Both methods compress and close off

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:27.270
the artery. In all cases of serious bleeding,
be sure to call the doctor.

00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:34.809
To return to digital pressure -- there are
several points on each side of the body where

00:05:34.809 --> 00:05:41.809
the arteries lie close to a bone. It's at
these points where pressure is most effective.

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:48.539
Arterial bleeding can be controlled on the
scalp and on the upper portion of the head

00:05:48.539 --> 00:05:54.009
by finger pressure just in front of the middle
portion of the ear.

00:05:54.009 --> 00:05:59.789
This stops the escape of blood from the arteries
serving this particular area.

00:05:59.789 --> 00:06:05.639
Blood flowing from a cut artery in the lower
portion of the face may be controlled by pressure

00:06:05.639 --> 00:06:11.519
on the underside of the jawbone, just in front
of the angle of the jaw. 

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:16.900
This cuts off the supply of blood to this portion of the face.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:23.100
Arterial bleeding from the neck can be controlled
by pressure on the bleeding side of the neck.

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:29.689
Anchor the thumb against the nape of the neck,
and with the fingers press alongside the windpipe

00:06:29.689 --> 00:06:32.409
toward the spinal column.

00:06:32.409 --> 00:06:37.389
Care must be taken not to press against the
windpipe.

00:06:37.389 --> 00:06:42.889
For bleeding around the shoulder or armpit,
bend the head slightly toward the bleeding

00:06:42.889 --> 00:06:49.889
side and bring firm pressure to bear with
the thumb behind the center of the collarbone.

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:54.960
The flow through this artery will thus be
effectively controlled.

00:06:54.980 --> 00:07:00.940
Arterial bleeding of the arm at any point
below the armpit may be controlled by pressure

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:05.480
on the blood vessel against the bone of the
upper arm,

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:09.320
thus compressing the artery and checking the flow of blood.

00:07:10.559 --> 00:07:16.559
If an artery is cut in the leg or thigh, bleeding
may be stopped by pressing firmly against

00:07:16.559 --> 00:07:23.360
the thighbone high up on the inner side of
the thigh, thus shutting off the blood supply

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:26.349
to the limb below this point.

00:07:26.349 --> 00:07:32.990
When bleeding from the arm or leg cannot be
controlled by digital pressure, use a tourniquet.

00:07:32.990 --> 00:07:39.860
A triangular bandage rolled into a cravat
makes an excellent tourniquet, as does a

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:47.920
roller bandage, stockings, a large handkerchief, even a necktie will do if nothing else is available,

00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:52.600
but never use anything such as
a wire or rope, 

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:56.980
which may cut into the skin and cause injury.

00:07:57.159 --> 00:08:04.519
When using a tourniquet, direct pressure on the artery is desirable. A small roll of bandage

00:08:04.659 --> 00:08:11.659
or folded gauze is excellent for this purpose. Never use anything hard or with sharp edges.

00:08:12.409 --> 00:08:16.029
Better to apply the tourniquet without a pad
at all.

00:08:16.029 --> 00:08:21.109
Tuck the roll of bandage or pad directly over
the artery. 

00:08:21.220 --> 00:08:27.720
Wrap whatever material you are using for a tourniquet around the limb twice.

00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:31.780
Tie a loose knot. 

00:08:33.180 --> 00:08:40.580
Place a short stick over this knot and tie it securely in place.

00:08:42.420 --> 00:08:49.260
Now, turn the stick so that the tourniquet
tightens. Twist the stick only enough to stop

00:08:49.410 --> 00:08:55.730
the bleeding. This causes the pad to press
firmly against the artery, forming a barrier

00:08:55.730 --> 00:08:58.740
past which the blood cannot flow.

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:04.050
A tourniquet must be loosened at regular intervals
of 15 minutes to avoid permanent damage to

00:09:04.050 --> 00:09:11.050
the tissues. If bleeding starts again, tighten
the tourniquet. Remember to loosen the tourniquet

00:09:11.110 --> 00:09:18.110
every 15 minutes and tighten it again only
if bleeding resumes.

00:09:18.370 --> 00:09:26.350
A condition known as shock may be present in all injuries and very frequently in severe bleeding.

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:32.160
If vital functions of the body are
slowed down, shock may result in death.

00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:40.240
Therefore, while waiting for the doctor, treat
the victim for shock immediately after stopping the bleeding.

00:09:41.170 --> 00:09:49.590
A person in shock is pale, has a cold clammy
skin, weak rapid pulse, and breathes irregularly.

00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:58.100
Keep him quiet, lying down, warm him, wrap him completely in blankets 

00:09:58.260 --> 00:10:03.720
or a coat or anything to keep him warm.

00:10:03.730 --> 00:10:09.110
Apply hot water bottles, carefully wrapped.
Bags of heated sand or salt.

00:10:09.140 --> 00:10:13.220
Take care, though, that you don't overdo it and burn the injured.

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:20.860
Another thing to remember in treating shock
is position. Keep the body flat

00:10:21.460 --> 00:10:29.020
and raise the foot of the bed, cot, or stretcher so the head is lower than the rest of the body.

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:35.970
If the injured is lying on something that
cannot be raised, keep his feet, legs, and thighs raised.

00:10:37.520 --> 00:10:42.960
Keep him lying quietly and unless
he coughs up blood or has difficulty in breathing

00:10:43.550 --> 00:10:49.000
or has a nosebleed, don't allow him to sit
up.

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:55.959
At this point, stimulants are important. One
of the most effective is aromatic squirts

00:10:55.959 --> 00:11:02.959
of ammonia, one teaspoonful in one half glass
of water. Hot coffee or tea also are excellent

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:08.740
stimulants and are valuable in supplying heat.

00:11:08.750 --> 00:11:14.959
If he is unconscious, never try to force him
to drink. An unconscious person may be revived

00:11:14.959 --> 00:11:20.759
by using a stimulant, which can be inhaled,
such as ammonia inhalant 

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:24.819
or aromatic squirts of ammonia on a handkerchief.

00:11:24.819 --> 00:11:31.819
Simply hold the handkerchief near the nose
for a few seconds, or use smelling salts.

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:39.060
Shock may be avoided if these steps are taken in time. 

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:45.780
So, those are the important things for you to do for a person suffering from shock.

00:11:45.790 --> 00:11:52.690
Keep him warm. Keep him lying quietly on his back with his head lower than his feet 

00:11:52.690 --> 00:11:54.650
and give him stimulants. 

00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:59.200
Now, these things sound simple and they are, 

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:07.440
but these simple first aid steps, taken before the doctor comes, have saved many a life.

00:12:07.470 --> 00:12:15.110
Now, let's go back and take up the second
type of bleeding, a wound in which a vein is cut.

00:12:16.040 --> 00:12:22.360
Now, here's the circulatory system again.
You have seen how the heart pumps blood through

00:12:22.360 --> 00:12:29.360
the arteries to all parts of the body. However, on the return trip the blood flows back to

00:12:29.449 --> 00:12:34.740
the heart through the other set of vessels,
the veins.

00:12:35.620 --> 00:12:41.740
Remember how an arterial wound bleeds, spurting with a lot of pressure behind it.

00:12:42.100 --> 00:12:46.120
A wound in which a vein is cut bleeds quite differently. 

00:12:46.240 --> 00:12:52.959
Blood escapes in a slow, steady stream, and with much less force.

00:12:52.959 --> 00:12:58.810
The best way to stop this type of bleeding
is by direct pressure on the bleeding point

00:12:58.810 --> 00:13:00.680
with a sterile dressing.

00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:07.680
Dressings are usually made of gauze and are
available in various sizes so that any wound

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:12.260
large or small can be completely covered.

00:13:12.260 --> 00:13:17.900
Because they come in direct contact with the
wound, dressings should be sterile.

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:21.720
That is, free of bacteria.

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:29.180
Sterile compresses are available in sealed
dustproof packages to prevent contamination.

00:13:30.439 --> 00:13:37.039
The compress is placed directly over the wound
and should be large enough to cover the entire wound.

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:43.139
The bandage is applied to hold the compress in place.

00:13:43.139 --> 00:13:47.959
There are a variety of bandages suitable for
all parts of the body.

00:13:48.140 --> 00:13:58.140
A very useful bandage for first aid work is the triangular bandage, which is merely a 36 or 40 inch square of

00:13:58.170 --> 00:14:03.360
cloth folded into a triangle or cut diagonally
into two triangles.

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:10.360
This can be used to hold a compress in place on the head, scalp, or face,

00:14:12.860 --> 00:14:18.580
to hold large dressings in place as, for example, on the chest,

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:28.060
to place over injuries of the hand or a foot, to bandage the shoulder and upper
arm.

00:14:29.480 --> 00:14:35.060
When folded as a cravat it can be used to
tie splints in place.

00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:43.220
It can also be used as an arm sling, and of course as a tourniquet.

00:14:43.400 --> 00:14:48.760
Do not apply a triangular bandage directly over a wound.

00:14:50.580 --> 00:14:56.700
Many times a roller bandage may be used where
a triangular bandage is not available.

00:14:56.820 --> 00:15:03.200
This type of bandage can be used to hold a sterile compress or dressing in place,

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:10.740
to hold splints in place, as a sling for the arm, and as a tourniquet.

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:18.580
Likewise, the roller bandage may be used in injuries to the arm,

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:26.060
to bandage an injured hand, in wounds of the leg, the ankle or foot,

00:15:26.200 --> 00:15:32.480
to bandage the shoulder and chest, and in wounds of the head.

00:15:32.490 --> 00:15:38.830
When using a roller bandage, hold it this
way and don't allow much of it to unroll at one time.

00:15:38.940 --> 00:15:41.819
In this manner you will always have control of it.

00:15:41.819 --> 00:15:46.779
Always keep in mind that any bandage should
be applied firmly enough to keep the compress

00:15:46.779 --> 00:15:53.779
in place, yet not so tight that it tends to
stop the circulation of the blood.

00:15:54.640 --> 00:16:02.800
For minor wounds, the adhesive compress bandage proves very convenient and it has many uses.

00:16:03.220 --> 00:16:09.320
Be sure to remove the protective covering
from the adhesive ends and do not touch the

00:16:09.329 --> 00:16:13.009
sterile gauze pad with the fingers.

00:16:13.880 --> 00:16:21.840
There are other types of injuries, which also
require dressings and sometimes bandaging.

00:16:22.340 --> 00:16:32.720
Among these are burns.  Searing steam, boiling water,

00:16:32.720 --> 00:16:38.680
 fire, a hot stove:

00:16:39.820 --> 00:16:45.300
these are a few of the major causes of painful burns and scalds.

00:16:46.440 --> 00:16:54.100
In the case of burns, there are several important
things to do: treat for shock, dress the burn,

00:16:54.160 --> 00:16:55.709
and call for a doctor.

00:16:56.900 --> 00:17:03.540
In severe burns, after treating for shock,
remove all loose clothing over the burn.

00:17:04.360 --> 00:17:11.540
If any of the clothing sticks to the burned skin, cut around it, but don't tear it off.

00:17:11.840 --> 00:17:16.800
Get medical attention at once.

00:17:16.809 --> 00:17:26.369
If ordinary baking soda or bicarbonate of
soda is available, make a solution or a paste with tepid water.

00:17:26.840 --> 00:17:31.700
If there is some water which
has already been boiled, use that.

00:17:32.480 --> 00:17:37.120
Apply the solution or paste gently over the
burn. 

00:17:38.760 --> 00:17:42.660
Bandage very lightly and carefully.

00:17:43.000 --> 00:17:49.620
If baking soda or any other approved burn
preparation is not available, cover burns

00:17:49.620 --> 00:17:56.540
with a sterile dressing, bandage lightly,
and wait for the doctor.

00:17:57.620 --> 00:18:04.980
Now, here are just a few don'ts. Try not to
break open a blister and 

00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:12.380
don't apply to a burn cotton, iodine, oils, or greases.

00:18:12.980 --> 00:18:19.080
But first aid treatment isn't limited to
dressing a wound or a burn or stopping the

00:18:19.080 --> 00:18:25.530
flow of blood. A person who knows first aid
can be of help in cases of unconsciousness.

00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:36.380
An overturned canoe, a running engine behind
closed garage doors, a hissing gas jet,

00:18:36.660 --> 00:18:43.440
a live electric wire, these tell what to look
for.

00:18:44.800 --> 00:18:51.240
Now, if in such cases breathing has stopped,
the person may die,

00:18:51.320 --> 00:19:00.600
but given artificial respiration immediately, his breathing may be restored and his life saved.

00:19:01.480 --> 00:19:07.600
Now, here's the proper way to give artificial
respiration. Loosen tight clothing.

00:19:07.960 --> 00:19:13.760
To roll him over, place one arm over his head, bend the other arm at the elbow, 

00:19:14.060 --> 00:19:18.340
and place the back of his hand beside his mouth.

00:19:18.340 --> 00:19:25.120
Roll him so that his head comes to rest on
that hand. Turn his head upward so that the

00:19:25.120 --> 00:19:32.120
nose and mouth are left free for breathing.
Make sure the mouth is clear and unobstructed.

00:19:32.490 --> 00:19:37.890
Remove dental plates or any substances which
may restrict breathing. 

00:19:38.260 --> 00:19:45.380
If the tongue has fallen backward, pull it forward. Straddle the victim's thigh.

00:19:45.390 --> 00:19:50.130
Now place the palms of your hands on the
victim's back. 

00:19:50.360 --> 00:19:57.280
Your fingers should follow the curve of the ribs, with the little finger right over the lowest rib.

00:19:57.290 --> 00:20:02.930
Place your knees below his hip bone, so that when you lean forward, your shoulders are directly

00:20:02.930 --> 00:20:08.910
over the small of his back. Be sure to assume a comfortable position.

00:20:08.910 --> 00:20:15.910
Swing forward slowly and without bending your elbows, apply the weight of your body 

00:20:16.060 --> 00:20:24.220
gradually on the victim. This is step one of artificial respiration and should take about two seconds,

00:20:24.220 --> 00:20:29.880
or the time you can count four--thus 1, 2,
3, 4.

00:20:30.340 --> 00:20:37.340
Now, release the pressure by swinging back
away from the victim. This is step two.

00:20:37.740 --> 00:20:42.960
This completes one cycle and should take about five seconds if done properly.

00:20:43.140 --> 00:20:47.780
Artificial respiration should be given in
a continuous operation 

00:20:47.920 --> 00:20:54.340
and with the proper timing: 1, 2, 3, 4.

00:20:54.340 --> 00:21:01.340
Remember the old song? Out goes the bad air,
in comes the good air. 

00:21:01.440 --> 00:21:06.320
Out goes the bad air, in comes the good air.

00:21:08.020 --> 00:21:15.440
In some cases, it is well worthwhile to continue this treatment for several hours before giving up hope.

00:21:15.580 --> 00:21:41.080
[Ticking clock sounds are heard as rescuer
continues administering artificial respiration.]

00:21:41.240 --> 00:21:44.620
If you become tired, have someone else take over,

00:21:44.620 --> 00:21:48.360
taking care that the rhythm is not interrupted or changed.

00:21:49.320 --> 00:21:53.960
Watch the victim carefully when his natural breathing returns.

00:21:54.320 --> 00:21:59.400
Recovery may be only temporary and he may again stop breathing.

00:21:59.660 --> 00:22:05.280
When this happens, resume artificial respiration immediately.

00:22:05.290 --> 00:22:10.740
Treat the victim for shock as soon as possible, and when he's breathing normally,

00:22:10.740 --> 00:22:14.910
give him a stimulant and keep him lying quietly until the doctor arrives.

00:22:15.760 --> 00:22:21.900
Now remember, in giving first aid to a person
whose breathing has stopped, start artificial

00:22:21.940 --> 00:22:29.640
respiration promptly. Not too forcibly and
not too rapidly, but with a definite rhythm.

00:22:30.030 --> 00:22:33.380
Call for an inhalator if available.

00:22:34.740 --> 00:22:40.380
And now we come to fractures, broken bones,
a rickety or broken stepladder,

00:22:41.320 --> 00:22:48.960
a carelessly placed toy, a bit of torn carpeting, stepping between parked cars. 

00:22:49.880 --> 00:22:54.800
These are but a few of the causes resulting in fractures of bones.

00:22:55.640 --> 00:23:00.460
When a bone is broken, it may be either a simple or a compound fracture. 

00:23:01.360 --> 00:23:03.800
Now, here is the difference.

00:23:04.320 --> 00:23:10.530
Here is an x-ray of a simple fracture. The
bone is broken, but the skin has not been pierced.

00:23:11.410 --> 00:23:16.110
The area around the broken bone is usually quite tender and painful. 

00:23:16.680 --> 00:23:21.300
Within a short time, it'll become discolored and swollen.

00:23:22.340 --> 00:23:28.300
Now, here is an x-ray of a compound fracture,
more serious than a simple fracture.

00:23:28.940 --> 00:23:34.860
There is a connecting wound to the surface in the immediate vicinity of the break.

00:23:35.120 --> 00:23:40.400
Bleeding may be severe. A tourniquet may be needed.

00:23:40.400 --> 00:23:47.020
When you suspect a fracture, call a doctor
immediately, then treat the victim for shock

00:23:47.020 --> 00:23:49.160
and make him comfortable,

00:23:49.160 --> 00:23:54.900
and finally if he must be moved or if a doctor isn't available, apply a splint. 

00:23:55.180 --> 00:24:03.640
If it's a compound fracture, stop any bleeding and dress the wound and apply a splint as in simple fracture.

00:24:04.980 --> 00:24:11.360
The purpose of a splint is to hold the limb
in a rigid position and prevent movement,

00:24:11.360 --> 00:24:14.100
which might aggravate the injury.

00:24:14.100 --> 00:24:21.100
An effective splint is a so-called fixed traction
type. A tourniquet is often placed around

00:24:21.250 --> 00:24:24.900
the injured member, to be ready for use if
bleeding occurs.

00:24:26.940 --> 00:24:35.140
For emergency purposes in the absence of a
traction splint, a board, rolled newspapers,

00:24:35.260 --> 00:24:41.600
pillow, or umbrella may serve as a splint.

00:24:41.780 --> 00:24:47.100
Regardless of the material used for splinting,
it should extend beyond the joints

00:24:47.200 --> 00:24:55.120
above and below the fracture. If possible, it should also be wide enough to give adequate support.

00:24:56.140 --> 00:25:02.120
Tie the splints securely, but not so tight
as to stop circulation of the blood.

00:25:02.120 --> 00:25:08.760
Examine the injured part frequently, say, every 15 minutes.

00:25:08.760 --> 00:25:15.080
If the injured part becomes too painful or
if circulation is being cut off,

00:25:15.180 --> 00:25:18.900
loosen the ties around the splint.

00:25:20.880 --> 00:25:25.900
Now, many times there is need of moving an injured person, either because a doctor isn't

00:25:26.040 --> 00:25:30.780
available or because of the location of the
accident.

00:25:30.780 --> 00:25:37.120
For instance, in the case of a victim who
has a fractured forearm, the transportation

00:25:37.120 --> 00:25:43.960
problem is relatively simple. The forearm
should be splinted before the victim is moved.

00:25:43.960 --> 00:25:49.820
However, the transportation of a victim with
a more serious fracture presents a greater problem.

00:25:51.520 --> 00:25:57.200
In fact, the victim should not be
moved unless absolutely necessary 

00:25:57.320 --> 00:26:00.120
or under the advice of a doctor.

00:26:01.440 --> 00:26:05.140
[ Horn blowing, little girl running down the
front walk of her house. ]

00:26:05.140 --> 00:26:12.560
How many times have you read of a well-meaning motorist lifting an injured child from the street?

00:26:12.580 --> 00:26:18.960
The very way the kind motorist lifts the child,
jackknifing the child into his car, 

00:26:19.080 --> 00:26:26.700
may cause broken ribs to act as jagged spears, which might easily puncture the internal organs.

00:26:28.780 --> 00:26:34.220
Whenever there is a fracture of the lower
limbs or other serious fractures,

00:26:34.360 --> 00:26:42.160
follow these simple rules. Only after a splint has been applied, use a stretcher. 

00:26:42.300 --> 00:26:50.460
A door or a wide board will do if a stretcher isn't available.

00:26:54.620 --> 00:27:01.220
Place the victim carefully onto the stretcher, taking great care not to twist or jar the body.

00:27:02.800 --> 00:27:10.860
Keep him lying flat. When carrying the
stretcher avoid unnecessary jarring or swinging,

00:27:10.870 --> 00:27:13.710
which might aggravate the injury.

00:27:13.710 --> 00:27:18.570
Remember, an injured person is at your mercy.

00:27:18.680 --> 00:27:24.280
Treat them as gently and as kindly as you would like to be treated. 

00:27:25.380 --> 00:27:29.960
Well, you have seen some of the essentials of first aid.

00:27:29.960 --> 00:27:36.880
Now, this doesn't make a doctor of you nor
a first aid expert, but a knowledge of

00:27:36.880 --> 00:27:45.340
first aid should enable you with practice to relieve suffering and perhaps save a life.

00:27:46.530 --> 00:27:53.530
When your opportunity will come or where it will be, nobody knows,

00:27:53.530 --> 00:27:58.840
but your help may be wanted, may be needed at any time.

00:28:00.020 --> 00:28:04.780
If you have not already done so, enroll for
a first aid course 

00:28:04.920 --> 00:28:09.400
and learn how to use your equipment properly.

00:28:10.480 --> 00:28:16.740
Now always keep in mind these simple but important
rules to follow before the doctor arrives.

00:28:19.020 --> 00:28:27.280
Know what to look for. Know what to do.
Know how to do it.

00:28:27.500 --> 00:28:33.880
And when you call the doctor, don't forget
to give him some details as to the nature

00:28:33.880 --> 00:28:41.980
or seriousness of the injury. And if you want to be a really good samaritan, 

00:28:41.980 --> 00:28:50.000
it's generally a pretty good idea to let the doctor know just where the injured person is.

00:28:50.000 --> 00:28:59.320
[Music]

